# Line endings and binary handling. Committed on purpose: this is the setting that makes the
# repo behave identically on the machine that clones it, which `core.autocrlf` cannot do because it
# lives in each person's own config.
#
# Why it was needed here: Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in its system config, which
# writes CRLF into the working copy while storing LF in the repository. The result was git warning
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" on almost every status run, and a
# 77-LF / 36-CRLF split across the files on disk. `eol=lf` overrides that per-repo, so the working
# copy stops being rewritten and a Linux or macOS contributor does not show up as having changed
# every line in the project.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Never run line-ending conversion over these. Rewriting a byte inside an image, font or audio
# file corrupts it silently — it still opens, and it is just wrong.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.mp4 binary
*.mp3 binary
*.flac binary

# Generated files. Marked so GitHub collapses them in a pull request diff instead of burying the
# real change under 100k lines of lockfile.
package-lock.json linguist-generated
src-tauri/Cargo.lock linguist-generated
src-tauri/gen/** linguist-generated
